C-BROWSER-DOWNLOADS-COOKIES-SERVICES-202.Problem:
M1.2 exposes low-level experimental engine primitives for cookies, storage and download hooks, while M1.3 currently contains only tabs and durable history services.
The engine primitive surface is intentionally too low-level to expose directly as product semantics:
cookie reads return raw values together with metadata;
low-level download contracts contain destination-path strings;
engine DownloadId represents an engine operation rather than a durable user-visible download record;
successful completion of engine I/O does not itself define durable artifact ownership;
Chromium cookie storage is authoritative for cookie contents, while user policy and service access rules belong outside the engine provider.
A product service layer is therefore required to establish separate authority, persistence, recovery, privacy and artifact boundaries without turning the CEF provider into a download manager or cookies product.

AcceptanceCriteria.Criterion:
Temporary provider staging data is either cleaned after successful
artifact handoff or remains under an explicitly bounded recovery policy;
cleanup never deletes a file outside the authorized staging root.
